            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">When should I start preparing for hurricane season?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Begin preparations in May, before the official start of June 1 hurricane season. Complete home hardening, supply purchases, insurance reviews, and evacuation route mapping while stores have inventory and weather is calm. Waiting until a storm is named leaves you competing for dwindling supplies with millions of other residents.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">Should I board up my windows with plywood or install shutters?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Plywood is the most affordable option and provides adequate protection when properly installed with concrete screws. Permanent shutters offer faster deployment and better protection but cost more upfront. Impact-resistant windows eliminate boarding entirely but represent the highest initial investment. Choose based on your budget, how often you face hurricane threats, and how quickly you need to protect your home.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">How do I know if I am in an evacuation zone?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Check your county emergency management agency website for evacuation zone maps. These maps divide areas into zones based on storm surge risk. Know your zone letter before hurricane season begins. When authorities issue evacuation orders, they reference these zones by letter. You can also call your local emergency management office to confirm your zone designation.</div></div>
            <div style="border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; overflow: hidden;"><div style="padding: 16px 20px; background: rgba(255,255,255,0.03); cursor: pointer;"><strong style="color: #fff;">What should I do during the hurricane?</strong></div><div style="padding: 0 20px 16px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.7); line-height: 1.8;">Stay in your designated interior shelter room away from all windows. Keep your NOAA weather radio on for updates. Never go outside to observe the storm, even during calm periods in the eye. If you hear a sudden roar or feel pressure changes, move to the most interior space and cover yourself with a mattress or heavy blankets. Stay sheltered until official all-clear announcements.</div></div>
